        <div class="pitfalls-grid">
          <div class="pitfall-item">
            <h4>Confusing Tons and Cubic Yards</h4>
            <p>
              A cubic yard is volume; a ton is weight.
              One cubic yard of standard gravel weighs 2,700 pounds (1.35 tons).
              Ordering 10 tons yields only 7.4 cubic yards.
              Always confirm whether your supplier quotes prices per cubic yard or per ton.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pitfall-item">
            <h4>Underestimating Bed Depth for Large Stone</h4>
            <p>
              Stone caliper dictates bed depth.
              A bed of 1-3" river rock installed only 2 inches deep will expose the black fabric beneath.
              As an aggregate rule of thumb, minimum bed depth should be at least 1.5 to 2 times the average stone size (or at least 1 times the maximum stone caliper). For 1-3" river rock (average 2"), maintain a 3" to 4" depth; for 3-5" large river stone (average 4"), install at 4" to 6" minimum.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pitfall-item">
            <h4>Ignoring Moisture Weight at the Quarry Scale</h4>
            <p>
              Aggregates stored in outdoor quarry stockpiles absorb rainwater.
              Wet crushed stone can weigh 5% to 8% more per cubic yard than dry material.
              When buying by certified scale weight after heavy rainfall, slight volumetric adjustments occur.
            </p>
